{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n6le03ob05re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n6le03ob05re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob05re2img01.jpg", "text": "<strong>Claire</strong>: There are some JavaScript statements that are conditionally executed, but I can't manage to understand how they work.<br/>\n<strong>Max</strong>: Well, I have been reading this hi-tech magazine that made a review on JavaScript for beginners. They talk about the different conditional statements that JavaScript users can work with when programming.<br/>\n<strong>Claire</strong>: It might come in handy to read the magazine. Would it be ok if I borrow it?<br/>\n<strong>Max</strong>: Sure, I have it right here. I can help you go through the concepts if you want.", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob05re2img02.jpg", "text": "<strong>Claire</strong>: That'll be great!  Let me read the first part. \u201cYou might be familiar with the conditional statements. In JavaScript, the 'if...else' statements are used to give the program directions on what and how statements should be executed depending on a condition. It might sound difficult, but if you write the code neatly, you won't have any trouble. JavaScript supports three forms of 'if...else' statements: the 'if' statement, the 'if...else' statement, and the 'if...else...if' statement. Let's take a look at them.\u201d<br/>\n<strong>Max</strong>: I'll continue. \u201cThe first one is the 'if' statement: this is the basic control statement which JavaScript relies on to make decisions. In this kind of statement, if the result is true, the given statement is executed, if it's false, it won't be executed. The second type of \u2018if' statement is the 'if...else' statement. In this case, the expression is evaluated and if it's true, the given statement in the 'if' block is executed. If it's false, the statement that was given in the 'else' block is executed.\u201d", "time": "31500"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob05re2img03.jpg", "text": "<strong>Claire</strong>: It seems easy. Let's check the third one. \u201cThe last one is the 'if...else...if' statement: it's the level-up of the 'if...else' statements. Here, JavaScript is able to make a decision out of a number of conditions. It can be described also as a series of 'if' statements. The statement is executed based on what the true condition is, and if no condition is true, the 'else' block is executed.\u201d <br/>\n<strong>Max</strong>: Easy, right?</br>\n<strong>Claire</strong>: Yes! Now I'll have to try it out to see if I can get the output right.", "time": "112500"}]}, "type": "slideshow"}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "type": "WLC", "questions": [], "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "img_path": "", "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can use conditional statements in JavaScript.   ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can list the different types of conditional statements in JavaScript. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about characteristics for the different types of conditional statements in JavaScript.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "When do you use a flow chart?  ", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": " What conditional statements would you implement in JavaScript?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partn